# Checks for a program's own assumptions. `assert-that` stops the run with the stdlib's `defect` when its condition is
# false, on every backend, the way a list read past the end does: a broken assumption is a stop, not an exception a
# caller handles (note/term/stdlib/semantics.md, "Two kinds of failure"). To print while debugging, `log/debug`; for the
# call stack, `error/trace`.
#
# RENAMED AND CUT 2026-10-02 (base-v1-0020): this module declared `inspect`, `print`, `pause` and `assert` with no
# bodies, so a call built clean and failed at run time.

note unstable

load @term/base/code/stop
  find abandon

# true when the condition holds; otherwise the run stops, saying what was assumed
task assert-that
  take condition, like boolean
  take message, like text
  like boolean
  fork test
    hook test
      read condition
    hook hold
      send back, true
  call abandon, text <assertion: {{message}}>
  send back, false
